On Tue, Nov 2, 2010 at 8:30 PM, Lindar <lindarthebard@yahoo.com> wrote:
> To add to this, yes there -is- a precedent that {se broda} is not the
> same as {selbroda} and it's even in common usage. {se kelci} vs.
> {selkei} vs. {nunkei} etc. etc.
>

  Whatever you believe about it being in "common usage", I see no
definition of selkei in jbovlaste, so it's not "official".  But I can
guess what it is -- a "se kelci".  QED :-)  A nunkei, OTOH cannot be a
selkei because a nunkei must be an event, whereas a selkei is not (and
if you are going to tell me that "Monopoly" is a nunkei, it's not.
Only a session of playing Monopoly is.  The game itself is either a se
kelci or a te nunkei.

